{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-10-08T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What if a forest could keep you guessing at every turn? At Puzzlewood, near Coleford in the Forest of Dean, England, winding paths lead through twisted trees, moss-covered rocks and deep stone ravines. Its unusual rock formations, known as scowles, are natural geological features that were exposed and modified over time and later exploited for iron ore mining during the Roman period and possibly earlier. In 1848, a worker discovered more than 3,000 Roman-era coins hidden in earthenware jars inside a rock cavity, turning the woodland into a real-life treasure hunt.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ForestofDean_ROW6726223892_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ForestofDean_ROW6726223892_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Moss-covered rocks in Puzzlewood, Forest of Dean, Gloucestershire, England","Copyright":"© Fulcanelli_AOS/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261007_ForestofDean\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Puzzlewood+England&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261007_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261007_ForestofDean"},"Ssd":"20261007","Name":"ForestofDean","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6726223892","FullDateString":"7 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What if a mountain range could show you its geological history in stripes? At Zhangye National Geopark in China, the rocks do exactly that. Designated as a UNESCO Global Geopark in 2020, Zhangye is in Gansu, a region linked to the ancient Silk Road. Here, ridges roll across the landscape in bands of red, orange, yellow, brown and other earthy tones. Known as the Danxia landform, this striking landscape was shaped by layers of sedimentary rock deposited over geological time. Tectonic forces later lifted and folded these layers, while weathering and erosion carved them into ridges, cliffs, gullies and peaks.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.DanxiaLandform_ROW6165298580_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.DanxiaLandform_ROW6165298580_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Danxia landform, Zhangye National Geopark, Gansu, China","Copyright":"© Weiquan Lin/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261006_DanxiaLandform\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Zhangye+National+Geopark&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261006_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261006_DanxiaLandform"},"Ssd":"20261006","Name":"DanxiaLandform","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6165298580","FullDateString":"6 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What can a 70-centimetre-tall bird teach us about life? Quite a lot. The Adélie penguin offers a lesson plan packed with resourcefulness, resilience and a little mischief. Found along the Antarctic coast, the species was named after Adèle, the wife of French explorer Jules Dumont d'Urville, during the expedition that encountered the penguins in 1840.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.AdelieTeacher_ROW3833533139_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.AdelieTeacher_ROW3833533139_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Adélie penguins, Antarctica","Copyright":"© Otto Plantema/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261005_AdelieTeacher\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Adelie+penguins&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261005_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261005_AdelieTeacher"},"Ssd":"20261005","Name":"AdelieTeacher","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3833533139","FullDateString":"5 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Castles usually come with towers, walls and at least one dramatic story. Château de Castelnaud in France has plenty of all three, plus medieval weapons. Rising around 150 metres above the Dordogne Valley, this 13th-century fortress commands views towards Château de Beynac and Périgord Noir. Its story is anything but quiet. During the Hundred Years' War, Castelnaud changed allegiance several times before French forces captured it after a three-week siege in 1442. Centuries later, the neglected fortress was used as a stone quarry before restoration began in 1965.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.CastelnaudPatrimoine_ROW3072375181_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.CastelnaudPatrimoine_ROW3072375181_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Château de Castelnaud overlooking the river Dordogne, France","Copyright":"© garethkirklandphotogrphy/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261004_CastelnaudPatrimoine\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Chateau+de+Castelnaud&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261004_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261004_CastelnaudPatrimoine"},"Ssd":"20261004","Name":"CastelnaudPatrimoine","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3072375181","FullDateString":"4 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"The season of big meals, bigger bellies and stocking up for winter is here. In Alaska, brown bears at Lake Clark National Park and Preserve make the most of the food still available in October. They feed on fish, sedges, berries, roots, clams and other foods to build up fat reserves. Some can weigh more than 450 kilograms before entering their dens for hibernation. That stored fat helps them survive the winter, when they do not eat or drink.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.GrizzlySwim_ROW2767752337_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.GrizzlySwim_ROW2767752337_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Brown bear in Silver Salmon Creek, Lake Clark National Park and Preserve, Alaska, United States","Copyright":"© Danny Green/Nature Picture Library","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261003_GrizzlySwim\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Brown+bear&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261003_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261003_GrizzlySwim"},"Ssd":"20261003","Name":"GrizzlySwim","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2767752337","FullDateString":"3 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Rivers have long played an important role in life across the world, supporting communities, wildlife and industry. Featured in today's image, the Chattooga River runs for about 92 kilometres across Georgia, North Carolina and South Carolina in the United States. Flowing through the Appalachian Mountains, it winds between steep, forested slopes, rocky gorges and cascading rapids before reaching Lake Tugaloo. The river remains one of the Southeast's few major free-flowing rivers and is known for its scenery, whitewater recreation and wildlife.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ChattoogaRiver_ROW2393025936_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ChattoogaRiver_ROW2393025936_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Chattooga River in the Appalachian Mountains, North Carolina, United States","Copyright":"© mtilghma/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261002_ChattoogaRiver\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Chattooga+River&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261002_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261002_ChattoogaRiver"},"Ssd":"20261002","Name":"ChattoogaRiver","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2393025936","FullDateString":"2 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In 1890, Yosemite became America's third national park. More than a century later, it still feels like a place that operates on geological time rather than human schedules. From Olmsted Point, the overlook in today's image, that timescale is impossible to miss. The smooth granite underfoot was sculpted by glaciers, while scattered boulders known as glacial erratics were carried here by ice and abandoned when it melted.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.OlmstedPoint_ROW5031185741_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.OlmstedPoint_ROW5031185741_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sunset from Olmsted Point, Yosemite National Park, California, United States","Copyright":"© Robb Hirsch/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261001_OlmstedPoint\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Yosemite+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261001_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261001_OlmstedPoint"},"Ssd":"20261001","Name":"OlmstedPoint","Market":"ROW","Hash":"5031185741","FullDateString":"1 out. 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"How do we know what Earth looked like long before humans appeared? Fossils provide many clues. Palaeontological discoveries help scientists reconstruct past environments, trace the evolution of life and learn about creatures that vanished millions of years ago. From dinosaurs to tiny marine organisms, these remnants of the past preserve snapshots of ancient worlds that no longer exist.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.AmmoniteFossils_ROW1149504413_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.AmmoniteFossils_ROW1149504413_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Ammonite fossils from the Jurassic Period","Copyright":"© J Nemchinova/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261014_AmmoniteFossils\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Ammonite+fossils&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261014_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261014_AmmoniteFossils"},"Ssd":"20261014","Name":"AmmoniteFossils","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1149504413","FullDateString":"14 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Water seems to be everywhere at Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia. Sixteen terraced lakes are linked by a network of waterfalls, streams and cascades that wind through forested limestone canyons. The constantly changing colours of the water, ranging from emerald green to deep blue, are shaped by minerals, sunlight and ever-changing reflections. Established in 1949, Plitvice Lakes is Croatia's oldest and largest national park. The landscape is famous for its tufa barriers, natural limestone formations created as mineral-rich water deposits calcium carbonate over centuries. These barriers continue to grow and change, gradually transforming the park's waterfalls and lakes.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.PlitviceAutumn_ROW0890292044_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.PlitviceAutumn_ROW0890292044_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Plitvice Lakes National Park, Croatia","Copyright":"© Jan Wlodarczyk/Alamy","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261013_PlitviceAutumn\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Plitvice+Lakes+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261013_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261013_PlitviceAutumn"},"Ssd":"20261013","Name":"PlitviceAutumn","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0890292044","FullDateString":"13 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What do you get when a railway, a lifeboat station and a seaside day out meet at the water's edge? Mumbles Pier. Stretching 255 metres into Swansea Bay, this Victorian landmark opened on 10 May 1898 and has been part of the Welsh coast ever since.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MumblesSwansea2026_ROW0047867144_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MumblesSwansea2026_ROW0047867144_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Mumbles Pier, Swansea, Wales","Copyright":"© Helen Hotson/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261012_MumblesSwansea2026\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Mumbles+Pier+Wales&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261012_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261012_MumblesSwansea2026"},"Ssd":"20261012","Name":"MumblesSwansea2026","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0047867144","FullDateString":"12 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Surrounded by a patchwork of red, orange and gold, Glade Creek Grist Mill stands above the rocky waters of Glade Creek in West Virginia's Babcock State Park, United States. Although it looks like a relic from the pioneer era, the mill is actually a carefully crafted tribute to Appalachia's milling heritage.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.BabcockSP_ROW9110416835_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.BabcockSP_ROW9110416835_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Glade Creek Grist Mill, Babcock State Park, West Virginia, United States","Copyright":"© dszc/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261011_BabcockSP\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Babcock+State+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261011_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261011_BabcockSP"},"Ssd":"20261011","Name":"BabcockSP","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9110416835","FullDateString":"11 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Today's image has a little airborne drama: double-crested cormorants are cruising above the waves of Monterey Bay, California, United States. Widespread across North America, these adaptable seabirds may migrate or remain year-round, depending on the population. Along California's coast, bays, wetlands and estuaries provide important habitat for birds travelling the Pacific Flyway.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.CormorantsFlight_ROW8571720617_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.CormorantsFlight_ROW8571720617_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Double-crested cormorants over Monterey Bay, California, United States","Copyright":"© Hiroya Minakuchi/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261010_CormorantsFlight\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Double+crested+cormorant&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261010_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261010_CormorantsFlight"},"Ssd":"20261010","Name":"CormorantsFlight","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8571720617","FullDateString":"10 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Just off the coast of Corsica, France, the Sanguinaires Islands rise from the Mediterranean as a small archipelago of four rugged, rocky islands. Viewed from a historic watchtower, they sit at the entrance to the Gulf of Ajaccio. Steep cliffs, centuries-old landmarks and sweeping sea views have drawn visitors to the area for generations. Though the farthest island lies only about 1.9 kilometres from shore, the archipelago feels surprisingly remote, surrounded by open water and shaped by wind, waves and salt spray.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.IlesSanguinaires_ROW8228238206_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.IlesSanguinaires_ROW8228238206_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"View of the Sanguinaires Islands from Corsica, France","Copyright":"© Francesco Riccardo Iacomino/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261009_IlesSanguinaires\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Sanguinaires+Islands+Corsica&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261009_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261009_IlesSanguinaires"},"Ssd":"20261009","Name":"IlesSanguinaires","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8228238206","FullDateString":"9 out. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Something has clearly crossed a line. Off the coast of Mayotte in the Indian Ocean, this octopus has adopted a defensive posture that says: whatever is approaching should reconsider its life choices.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MayotteOctopus_ROW7337263277_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MayotteOctopus_ROW7337263277_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Octopus in defensive posture, Mayotte, Indian Ocean","Copyright":"© Gabr